PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The least plausible explanation is H. This option fabricates an entirely unsupported esoteric/sexual reinterpretation (linking the circles to Kujiho, "nine holes," yin-yang erotic rituals, and chakra-style energy amplification) that has no grounding in the historical Bansenshukai, known ninja manuals, or period sources. It retrofits modern fantasy tropes onto the blanks rather than offering a credible historical or textual mechanism. All other options, while varying in likelihood, at least describe plausible real-world processes (editorial choice, moral censorship, physical wear, state secrecy, oral mnemonics, or invisible-ink copying conventions) consistent with 17th-century Japanese scribal practices and the documented transmission of the text.

(The other choices can be ranked by decreasing plausibility—G and B being the strongest on historical and material grounds—but the task only requires identifying the single least plausible one.)

1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The core issue is identifying the *least* plausible of the eight options given the fixed historical facts: the Bansenshukan was compiled in 1676 by Fujibayashi, the original is lost, and every surviving copy shows the identical pattern of filled and blank circles in the kunoichi section.  

Option C is the weakest on both chronology and internal logic. Lady Saigō died in 1589—nearly a century before the text existed—so she could not have used “techniques” from a book written long after her death. The claim that the Oniwaban or anyone else would have redacted a 1676 manuscript to protect the legitimacy of a regime whose founder she had already served is therefore chronologically impossible and rests on an invented causal chain. All other choices, while speculative, at least operate inside the correct century and invoke mechanisms (moral censorship, physical wear, mnemonic placeholders, later state control of copies, etc.) that do not contradict the known timeline outright. Hence C is the least plausible explanation.

